    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-01", "difficulty": "EASY", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 2,
     "question": "Expand (4m + 3n)^2.",
     "answer": "(4m)^2 + 2·(4m)·(3n) + (3n)^2 = 16m^2 + 24mn + 9n^2.",
     "explanation": "Square-of-sum identity with a = 4m, b = 3n."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-01", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 2,
     "question": "Use the (a + b)^2 identity to compute 105^2 mentally; show the steps.",
     "answer": "105^2 = (100 + 5)^2 = 100^2 + 2·100·5 + 5^2 = 10000 + 1000 + 25 = 11025.",
     "explanation": "Pick a = 100, b = 5 — easy mental arithmetic."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-02", "difficulty": "EASY", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 2,
     "question": "Compute 198 × 202 using a special-product identity.",
     "answer": "(200 − 2)(200 + 2) = 200^2 − 2^2 = 40000 − 4 = 39996.",
     "explanation": "Difference-of-squares: a = 200, b = 2."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-04", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 3,
     "question": "Expand (2y − 1)^3 and write the result in standard form.",
     "answer": "(2y)^3 − 3(2y)^2(1) + 3(2y)(1)^2 − 1^3 = 8y^3 − 12y^2 + 6y − 1.",
     "explanation": "Apply (a − b)^3 with a = 2y, b = 1."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-05", "difficulty": "EASY", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 2,
     "question": "Factor 7p^2 q + 14 p q^2 by taking out the common factor.",
     "answer": "HCF of 7p^2 q and 14pq^2 is 7pq. So 7p^2 q + 14pq^2 = 7pq(p + 2q).",
     "explanation": "Numerical HCF 7; variable HCF pq; quotients p and 2q."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-06", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 3,
     "question": "Factor by regrouping: 6ab + 3a + 4b + 2.",
     "answer": "Group: (6ab + 3a) + (4b + 2) = 3a(2b + 1) + 2(2b + 1) = (2b + 1)(3a + 2).",
     "explanation": "Pair terms so each group shares a binomial factor (2b + 1)."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-07", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 3,
     "question": "Factor x^2 + 9x + 20 and then verify your answer by re-multiplying.",
     "answer": "Need two numbers with sum 9 and product 20: (4, 5). So x^2 + 9x + 20 = (x + 4)(x + 5). Verify: (x + 4)(x + 5) = x^2 + 5x + 4x + 20 = x^2 + 9x + 20 ✓.",
     "explanation": "Find pair, factor, then check by FOIL."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-07", "difficulty": "HARD", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 3,
     "question": "Factor 6x^2 + x − 12 by splitting the middle term.",
     "answer": "a·c = −72; need sum 1 and product −72 → (9, −8). Split: 6x^2 + 9x − 8x − 12 = 3x(2x + 3) − 4(2x + 3) = (2x + 3)(3x − 4).",
     "explanation": "After splitting, group and pull out a common binomial factor."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-08", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 3,
     "question": "Factor 50x^2 − 32y^2 completely.",
     "answer": "Common factor 2: 50x^2 − 32y^2 = 2(25x^2 − 16y^2) = 2((5x)^2 − (4y)^2) = 2(5x − 4y)(5x + 4y).",
     "explanation": "Always pull out the common factor first; then apply difference of squares."},
    {"type": "SHORT_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-08", "difficulty": "MEDIUM", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 3,
     "question": "Factor 8x^3 + 125 using the sum-of-cubes identity.",
     "answer": "(2x)^3 + 5^3 = (2x + 5)((2x)^2 − (2x)(5) + 5^2) = (2x + 5)(4x^2 − 10x + 25).",
     "explanation": "Sum-of-cubes identity with a = 2x, b = 5."},

    {"type": "LONG_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-07", "difficulty": "HARD", "cognitive_level": "APPLY", "marks": 5,
     "question": "Factor 12x^2 + 11x − 5 by splitting the middle term, showing every step. Verify your factorisation by multiplying out.",
     "answer": "a·c = 12·(−5) = −60. Need two numbers with sum 11 and product −60: try (15, −4): 15 + (−4) = 11 ✓, 15·(−4) = −60 ✓. Split: 12x^2 + 15x − 4x − 5. Group: 3x(4x + 5) − 1(4x + 5) = (4x + 5)(3x − 1). Verify: (4x + 5)(3x − 1) = 12x^2 − 4x + 15x − 5 = 12x^2 + 11x − 5 ✓.",
     "explanation": "Split-the-middle-term works for any quadratic ax^2 + bx + c by finding sum b and product a·c."},
    {"type": "LONG_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-08", "difficulty": "HARD", "cognitive_level": "ANALYZE", "marks": 5,
     "question": "Factor x^4 − 16 completely. Identify each pattern you used.",
     "answer": "Step 1 (difference of squares): x^4 − 16 = (x^2)^2 − 4^2 = (x^2 − 4)(x^2 + 4). Step 2 (difference of squares again on x^2 − 4): x^2 − 4 = (x − 2)(x + 2). Note x^2 + 4 is a SUM of squares with no real factorisation. Final: x^4 − 16 = (x − 2)(x + 2)(x^2 + 4).",
     "explanation": "Apply difference of squares iteratively until each factor is irreducible over the reals."},
    {"type": "LONG_ANSWER", "outcome_code": "10-NIOS-MATH-SPF-04", "difficulty": "HARD", "cognitive_level": "EVALUATE", "marks": 5,
     "question": "If a + b = 6 and ab = 4, find (i) a^2 + b^2, (ii) a^3 + b^3 and (iii) (a − b)^2.",
     "answer": "(i) a^2 + b^2 = (a + b)^2 − 2ab = 36 − 8 = 28. (ii) a^3 + b^3 = (a + b)((a + b)^2 − 3ab) = 6·(36 − 12) = 6·24 = 144. (iii) (a − b)^2 = a^2 − 2ab + b^2 = (a^2 + b^2) − 2ab = 28 − 8 = 20.",
     "explanation": "All three follow from the special-product identities with the given symmetric data a + b and ab."}
  ]
}
